from sleeper hits like it comes at night  to box office record breakers like it  and get out  2017 has been a great year for horror  but for every high-profile killer Klown  there are a dozen low-key horror movies  that slip straight onto DVD or more  frequently these days video on demand  here are the underappreciated horror  movies that you may have missed in 2017  the devil's candy mixing heavy metal and  horror The Devil's candy follows a  struggling artist played with an  unsettling creepiness by ethan embry who  moves his family out to rural Texas when  the previous owner of their new house  starts making sinister nights and visits  the family's already turbulent lifestyle  descends into horror The Devil's candy  made the rounds at horror festivals in  2015 but it wasn't until 2017 that the  film got a more widespread release via  video on-demand services written and  directed by Sean Byrne whose previous  film was the equally unique horror flick  called the loved ones  The Devil's candy plays with beams from  old school of cult films while  confidently slipping into its own modern  take on the genre just the Gus  [Applause]  the evil within it's impossible to talk  about the evil within without mentioning  writer-director and Righetti who spent  more than a decade meticulously crafting  this schizophrenic passion project  before passing away in 2015 two years  before the film was released but he left  us with a very well-made highly visual  horror movie that will surely leave  viewers with some very specific  nightmares  the evil would then tells the story of a  mentally handicapped man named Dennis  played by Fredrik Koehler who lives with  his brother played by Sean Patrick  Flanery after Dennis's reflection starts  telling him to kill people things go  downhill pretty quickly to put it mildly  yeah that's good waste in it again  Berlin Syndrome Stockholm Syndrome is a  disturbing condition in which someone  who's been kidnapped develops a sense of  affection for the kidnapper the film for  Lynde syndrome plays with that idea by  following an Australian tourist who has  a one-night stand with a man in Berlin  only to find herself locked in his  apartment the struggle that follows is  pure psychological horror that dives  deep into the mindset of survival and  the subtleties of psychosis all anchored  by a phenomenal performance by  Australian actress Teresa Palmer at a  phone pounding musical score sometimes  touching sometimes brutal for a Lynne  syndrome is definitely a trip abroad  that you won't forget anytime soon the  black coats daughter  writer-director aux perkins slow-burn  possession film the black coats daughter  was made in 2015 but didn't secure a  release until 2017  starring American horror stories Emma  Roberts and Dexter's James remar the  film centers on two girls who are left  at their prep school after the rest of  the kids go home for vacation it turns  out that both girls are hiding a secret  an events start to spiral out of their  control in the worst possible way if  you're looking for a Gore fest filled  with jump scares  you're better off looking elsewhere but  if you want to spend your night with an  atmospheric horror film that bleeds  tension cue this one up it's fun to be  scared sometimes a dark song you  couldn't ask for a more powerful  directorial debut than a dark song and  Liam Gavin who pulled double duty is  both writer and director on this smart  engaging portrayal of ritual black magic  it's definitely a director to look for  in the future  Gavin crafted a surreal minimalist film  that may be one of the most technically  detailed occult movies of the last  decade if not ever  there aren't any jump scares very little  blood but the build up over at dark  songs 100 minute timeline is just as  satisfying as the ultimate climax dig  two graves  not your typical horror movie dig two  graves is more of a hard-boiled revenge  tale the story centers on a young girl  who makes a pact with a group of gypsy  mountain men to bring her brother back  from the dead if she's willing to pay  the price this indie thriller premiered  at the New Orleans Film Festival in  October 2014 then quietly sat in the  shadows until it was released online in  2017 but it was certainly worth the wait  dig two graves Glen's revenge thriller  fantasy and horror themes into one  twisted tale of a girl who may have to  make the ultimate sacrifice for her  family lakebottom while it may look like  a run-of-the-mill Screamfest Lake  bottoms twist is selling itself as a  teen slasher flick when it manages to be  so much more if you've never heard of it  don't beat yourself up after a short  festival run this Finnish horror  released slept quietly on to shudder a  video-on-demand service specializing in  horror movies virtually ensuring that  only a handful of people outside of  Finland would ever lay eyes on it but  for horror purists lakebottom is  definitely worth tracking down the void  Jeremy Gillespie and Stephen Coe stansky  first teamed up to write and direct the  2011 horror comedy Father's Day a neo  grindhouse tribute to the ultra violent  exploitation film genre that hit its  peak in the 70s their 2017 follow up the  void uses the same genre specific cues  as that film only this time to pay  tribute to cult eighties body horror the  void is messy weird and a ton of fun it  focuses on a police officer played by  Aaron Poole who finds himself trapped at  a hospital when mysterious hooded  figures surround the building but while  staying inside seems safe he soon finds  that the greatest terror is living in  the bowels of the hospital itself it's  disgusting like Carrie levels of blood  like very gross house on Willow Street  admittedly one of the weaker entries on  this list house on Willow Street may  fall a bit short of the mark and pacing  but the premise and visual design more  than make up for its flaws the film is  about two crooks who kidnap a young  woman in the hopes of getting a ransom  from her rich father little did they  know their victim is possessed and soon  she begins to turn the tables on her  kidnappers house on Willow Street  doesn't do much to defies genre  conventions but it serves very well as a  bloody fun horror romp for a lazy  evening the dark tapes at this point  we've had our fill of anthology films  that fad arguably kicked off with 2012's  VHS I'm sure there's a reasonable  explanation since then we've had the  ABCs of death  a Christmas horror story holidays and  tales of Halloween to name a few  so when an anthology film comes along  that not only exceeds expectations but  shatters them completely it's worth a  mention the dark tapes successfully  ratchets up the horror while avoiding  all the pitfalls that have doomed so  many other entries in this genre if you  want to be grossed out by alien monsters  and scared senseless by paranormal  visitors the dark tapes is the perfect  choice and while we're on the subject of  anthologies 2017 s X X as another  quality entry in this genre and unlike  the dark tapes that ones available on  Netflix whoa for some truths thanks for  watching click the looper icon to  subscribe to our YouTube channel let's  check out all this cool stuff we know  you love to  [Music]
 

